  "query_preview": "..Moisture has been streaming into southern Arizona\nwith PWAT values ranging from 1.4\" in western Pima County to\n1.0\" in Tucson. East of Tucson PWATs are still below an inch,\nbut will increase tonight into tomorrow. Aloft broad high\npressure is centered over west Texas with troughing well to the\nnorth. Arizona will stay under southerly flow aloft into the\nweekend which will keep moisture values up. Current radar and\nsatellite show cloudy skies with a few virga showers west of\nTucson. This activity will continue through the afternoon with\nlittle to no measurable rain expected. Tonight a couple HREF\nmember show weak showers east of Tucson, but again will most\nlikely be virga if it forms. Rain chances increase Saturday with\nmost activity staying east of I-19 and south of I-10 along with\nthe White Mountains. Tucson and southern Pinal County will be on\nthe northern periphery of storm chances. The main threat will \nbe gusty outflow winds with the 12/12Z HREF showing a 30-50 \npercent chance for winds in excess of 30 kts from Tucson south \nand eastward. \n\nSunday and Monday will see similar coverage and PoPs to\nSaturday, although Sunday Pops extend a little further west and\nnorth. Sunday looks to be the most active day. During this time\nthe ridge shifts westward slowly and a couple shortwaves move \nthrough the Rockies. This begins to turn winds aloft westerly \nwith moisture beginning to decrease late Monday. By Tuesday PWAT\nvalues decrease to 0.75\"-1.0\". However this will still be \nenough for isolated activity mainly south of Tucson Tuesday \nthrough the end of the work week. \n\nTemperatures will hover around normal each day into next week\nwith afternoon breezes. Winds will be strongest in the Gila\nRiver Valley. Still a little ways off but there is a chance for\nelevated fire weather conditions in the Gila River Valley next\nweek Wednesday and Thursday.",
